#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""pro1x-stream-webrtc [--raw WxH@FPS] [--interactive] PORT COMMAND... -- VP8-COMMAND... -- serve by WebRTC.
pro1x-stream-webrtc udp HOST PORT WxH@FPS COMMAND... -- send MPEG-TS by UDP.

With --interactive (the interactive stream, docs/interactive-stream.md), the
page also sends touches and keys back over a WebRTC data channel, which
virtual devices (uinput) play on the Pro1x: by default a multi-touch screen,
so apps get touches as from the Pro1x's own; in mouse mode a pointer (a tap
clicks, a drag drags, a long press right-clicks, two fingers scroll).  Its
buttons open the browser's keyboard, the Pro1x's own (stevia, for special
keys), its quick actions, launcher and window switcher.  The page can draw the video
on a canvas instead of a video element, shows what the browser reports
(screen size, codec, frames, delay), and logs it and the events it gets to
~/.local/state/pro1x/interactive-stream.log, for testing a car's browser.
Its SET panel, kept by the browser: the buffer before showing (smoother but
later), the sound's offset against the picture, a resync, and the H.264 bit
rate, changed on the phone's encoder while it runs.  The last button folds
the others away.

The sound is what the phone plays (the default output's monitor), as Opus.
A browser starts the page muted: the first touch turns the sound on, or the
SOUND button.

COMMAND writes H.264 (constrained baseline) to its standard output.  With a size
and rate it writes raw NV12 frames instead, for the hardware H.264 encoder.
VP8-COMMAND writes VP8 in IVF.  One of them
runs while a viewer is connected.  A browser gets a page from
http://ADDRESS:PORT/ and plays the video with no application; one viewer at a
time, and a new one replaces the last.

The page tries H.264, which costs the phone less, then VP8 if no picture comes:
LibreWolf lists H.264 but needs media.gmp-gmpopenh264.enabled to decode it, and
CEF (Nyxt) has no decoder.  VP8 is encoded in software: this encoder has no VP8.

The Pro1x makes the WebRTC offer and the browser answers, both by plain HTTP.
This way the browser takes the payload type GStreamer chose, and no signalling
server is needed.  ICE uses local addresses only.

State: VP8 played in a browser (author, 2026-11-10).  The hardware path encodes
real screen frames on the phone; not yet seen in a browser.
"""

